ll,{\"moduleIds\":\"$6f:props:children:0:props:moduleIds\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"w-full grid grid-cols-12 @container max-w-container multi-columns:px-0 multi-columns:flex\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"col-span-12 @md:col-span-6 @md:col-start-4 full-grid-content:@md:col-span-12 full-grid-content:@md:col-start-1 max-w-none prose\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"p\",\"0\",{\"className\":\"mb-sm last:mb-0\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$1\",\"0\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",\"text-end-0\",{\"children\":[\"Throughout the development of OpenAI o3, we’ve observed that large-scale reinforcement learning exhibits the same \"]}]]}],[\"$\",\"b\",\"1\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",\"text-end-0\",{\"children\":[\"“more compute = better performance\"]}]]}],[\"$\",\"$1\",\"2\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",\"text-end-0\",{\"children\":[\"” trend observed in GPT‑series pretraining. By retracing the scaling path—this time in RL—we’ve pushed an additional order of magnitude in both training compute and inference-time reasoning, yet still see clear performance gains, validating that the models’ performance continues to improve the more they’re allowed to think. At equal latency and cost with OpenAI o1, o3 delivers higher performance in ChatGPT—and we've validated that if we let it think longer, its performance keeps climbing.\"]}]]}]]}],[\"$\",\"p\",\"1\",{\"className\":\"mb-sm last:mb-0\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$1\",\"0\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",\"text-end-0\",{\"children\":[\"We also trained both models to\"]}]]}],[\"$\",\"b\",\"1\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",\"text-end-0\",{\"children\":[\" use tools through reinforcement learning\"]}]]}],[\"$\",\"$1\",\"2\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",\"text-end-0\",{\"children\":[\"—teaching them not just how to use tools, but to reason about when to use them. Their ability to deploy tools based on desired outcomes makes them more capable in open-ended situations—particularly those involving visual reasoning and multi-step workflows. This improvement is reflected both in academic benchmarks and real-world tasks, as reported by early testers. \"]}]]}]]}]]}]}]]}]]}]}]]}]]}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"73:[\"$\",\"$1\",\"5mc63Ru8DPBHRVJVY7vShf\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L6a\",null,{\"moduleIds\":\"$12:props:children:props:children:props:children:0:props:children:2:props:children:0:props:children:0:props:moduleIds\"}],[\"$\",\"section\",null,{\"className\":\"group/component-group @container\",\"data-multi-columns\":\"$undefined\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"mb-xs @md:mb-sm\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L8f\",null,{\"id\":\"1wmALOp5JWtL9fmrxJwNp2\",\"text\":\"Thinking with images\",\"headlineStyleDefault\":\"Heading 3\",\"headlineTag\":\"h2\",\"omitFromTableOfContents\":false,\"__typename\":\"componentContentHeading\",\"contentfulDebugAttributes\":{},\"children\":\"$undefined\"}]}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"@container grid w-full grid-cols-12\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"col-span-12 grid w-full grid-cols-1 items-stretch gap-lg @md:gap-xl\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$1\",\"3ktd9ZnL6GhYRujN4SDb3d\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L6a\",null,{\"moduleIds\":[2090]}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"multi-columns:flex multi-columns:px-0 @container grid w-full grid-cols-12 max-w-container\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"multi-columns:w-full col-span-12 @md:col-span-10 @md:col-start-2\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"gap-3xs grid size-full grid-cols-1 @md:grid-cols-1 max-w-container p-0\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L6c\",\"1Nnrlar34rsvKkwTNC3F2w\",{\"asset\":{\"title\":\"oai-thinking-with-images\",\"description\":\"\",\"file\":{\"url\":\"//images.ctfassets.net/kftzwdyauwt9/5xCw1KgHHs0BQI0Fz1bOLM/1ca4ddcca658fddd65815dd3aa288773/thinking_with_images_static.png\",\"details\":{\"size\":670154,\"image\":{\"width\":2257,\"height\":1447}},\"fileName\":\"thinking with images static.png\",\"contentType\":\"image/png\"}},\"altText\":\"ChatGPT thinking with images\",\"videoShowMuteButton\":true,\"showEmbeddedVideoControls\":false,\"captionAlignment\":\"content-grid\",\"truncateCaption\":true,\"centerCaption\":false,\"copyToClipboard\":false,\"id\":\"1Nnrlar34rsvKkwTNC3F2w\",\"__typename\":\"mediaItem\",\"contentfulDebugAttributes\":\"$undefined\",\"className\":\"flex flex-col\",\"lineClamp\":\"line-clamp-2\",\"borderRadius\":\"$undefined\",\"isVisible\":true,\"fullWidthCaption\":true}]]}]}]}]]}],[\"$\",\"$1\",\"4VCuP116tjvFrZbyMMUFyo\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L6a\",null,{\"moduleIds\":\"$6f:props:children:0:props:moduleIds\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"w-full grid grid-cols-12 @container max-w-container multi-columns:px-0 multi-columns:flex\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"col-span-12 @md:col-span-6 @md:col-start-4 full-grid-content:@md:col-span-12 full-grid-content:@md:col-start-1 max-w-none prose\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"p\",\"0\",{\"className\":\"mb-sm last:mb-0\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$1\",\"0\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",\"text-end-0\",{\"children\":[\"For the first time, these models can integrate images directly into their chain of thought. They don’t just see an image—they think with it. This unlocks a new class of problem-solving that blends visual and textual reasoning, reflected in their state-of-the-art performance across multimodal benchmarks.\"]}]]}]]}],[\"$\",\"p\",\"1\",{\"className\":\"mb-sm last:mb-0\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$1\",\"0\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",\"text-end-0\",{\"children\":[\"People can upload a photo of a whiteboard, a textbook diagram, or a hand-drawn sketch, and the model can interpret it—even if the image is blurry, reversed, or low quality. With tool use, the models can manipulate images on the fly—rotating, zooming, or transforming them as part of their reasoning process.\"]}]]}]]}],[\"$\",\"p\",\"2\",{\"className\":\"mb-sm last:mb-0\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$1\",\"0\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",\"text-end-0\",{\"children\":[\"These models deliver best-in-class accuracy on visual perception tasks, enabling it to solve questions that were previously out of reach. Check out the \"]}]]}],[\"$\",\"$L8d\",\"1\",{\"href\":\"/index/thinking-with-images\",\"external\":false,\"className\":\"underline-offset-[0.25rem] underline decoration-1 hover:text-primary-60 text-primary-100\",\"children\":[[[\"$\",\"$1\",\"0\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",\"text-end-0\",{\"children\":[\"visual reasoning research blog\"]}]]}]],\"⁠\"]}],[\"$\",\"$1\",\"2\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",\"text-end-0\",{\"children\":[\" to learn more.\"]}]]}]]}]]}]}]]}]]}]}]]}]]}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"74:[\"$\",\"$1\",\"6QyaFvnRrazHT0siXcJpKL\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L6a\",null,{\"moduleIds\":\"$12:props:children:props:children:props:children:0:props:children:2:props:children:0:props:children:0:props:moduleIds\"}],[\"$\",\"section\",null,{\"className\":\"group/component-group @container\",\"data-multi-columns\":\"$undefined\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"mb-xs @md:mb-sm\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L8f\",null,{\"id\":\"2q5P6T5PhHnxXPJjrFKwqW\",\"text\":\"Toward agentic tool use\",\"headlineStyleDefault\":\"Heading 3\",\"headlineTag\":\"h2\",\"omitFromTableOfContents\":false,\"__typename\":\"componentContentHeading\",\"contentfulDebugAttributes\":{},\"children\":\"$undefined\"}]}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"@container grid w-full grid-cols-12\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"col-span-12 grid w-full grid-cols-1 items-stretch gap-lg @md:gap-xl\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$1\",\"1XNRLTL6obASvfbrPMp9gW\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L6a\",null,{\"moduleIds\":\"$6f:props:children:0:props:moduleIds\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"w-full grid grid-cols-12 @container max-w-container multi-columns:px-0 multi-columns:flex\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"col-span-12 @md:col-span-6 @md:col-start-4 full-grid-content:@md:col-span-12 full-grid-content:@md:col-start-1 max-w-none prose\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"p\",\"0\",{\"className\":\"mb-sm last:mb-0\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$1\",\"0\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",\"text-end-0\",{\"children\":[\"OpenAI o3 and o4-mini have full access to tools within ChatGPT, as well as your own custom tools via function calling in the API. These models are trained to reason about how to solve problems, choosing when and how to use tools to produce detailed and thoughtful answers in the right output formats quickly—typically in under a minute.\"]}]]}]]}],[\"$\",\"p\",\"1\",{\"className\":\"mb-sm last:mb-0\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$1\",\"0\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",\"text-end-0\",{\"children\":[\"For example, a user might ask: “How will summer energy usage in California compare to last year?” The model can search the web for public utility data, write Python code to build a forecast, generate a graph or image, and explain the key factors behind the prediction, chaining together multiple tool calls. Reasoning allows the models to react and pivot as needed to information it encounters. For example, they can search the web multiple times with the help of search providers, look at results, and try new searches if they need more info. \"]}]]}]]}],[\"$\",\"p\",\"2\",{\"className\":\"mb-sm last:mb-0\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$1\",\"0\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",\"text-end-0\",{\"children\":[\"This flexible, strategic approach allows the models to tackle tasks that require access to up-to-date information beyond the model’s built-in knowledge, extended reasoning, synthesis, and output generation across modalities.\"]}]]}]]}],[\"$\",\"p\",\"3\",{\"className\":\"mb-sm last:mb-0\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"i\",\"0\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",\"text-end-0\",{\"children\":[\"All examples were completed with OpenAI o3.\"]}]]}]]}]]}]}]]}],[\"$\",\"$1\",\"3zX840w5sGfCQf4j84FuEQ\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L6a\",null,{\"moduleIds\":[\"17801189514031459093\"]}],[\"$\",\"$L98\",null,{\"header\":\"$undefined\",\"activeTab\":\"$undefined\",\"tabs\":[{\"content\":[\"$\",\"$1\",\"2tBy6ysnjwue4Ls4SMCHrM\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L6a\",null,{\"moduleIds\":[\"15747793709190119808\"]}],[\"$\",\"$L99\",null,{\"id\":\"2tBy6ysnjwue4Ls4SMCHrM\",\"conversations\":[{\"headline\":\"OpenAI o3\",\"messages\":[{\"variant\":\"input\",\"messageText\":{\"nodeType\":\"document\",\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\",\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"nodeType\":\"text\",\"value\":\"Solve the following very hard math problem using your in-built knowledge of the literature:\",\"marks\":[],\"data\":{}}]},{\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\",\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"nodeType\":\"text\",\"value\":\"Construct a degree 19 polynomial \\\\ (p(x)) \\\\ in \\\\ mathbb{C}[x] such that \\\\ (X := \\\\ {\\\\, p(x) = p(y) \\\\, \\\\} \\\\ subset \\\\ mathbb{P}^{1} \\\\ times \\\\ mathbb{P}^{1}\\\\ ) has at least 3 (but not all linear) irreducible components over \\\\ mathbb{C}. Choose \\\\ (p(x)\\\\ ) to be odd, monic, have real coefficients and linear coefficient \\\\ (-19\\\\ ) and calculate \\\\ (p(19)\\\\ ).\",\"marks\":[],\"data\":{}}]}]},\"autoExpandContext\":false,\"id\":\"6aIJczdXbX5KGWCG7kgSdI\",\"__typename\":\"chatGPTMessage\"},{\"variant\":\"response\",\"messageText\":{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\"A convenient way to force many factors in\\n$$p(x) \\\\;-\\\\; p(y)$$ is to use a \",\"nodeType\":\"text\"},{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[{\"type\":\"bold\"}],\"value\":\"Dickson polynomial\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"},{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\" of the first kind.\\nFor a parameter $$a \\\\in \\\\mathbb{C}$$ in math these are defined by\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\"$$$D_0\\\\!\\\\left(x,\\\\, a\\\\right) = 2,\\\\;\\nD_1\\\\!\\\\left(x,\\\\, a\\\\right) = x,\\\\;\\nD_n\\\\!\\\\left(x,\\\\, a\\\\right) = x\\\\,D_{n-1}\\\\!\\\\left(x,\\\\, a\\\\right)\\n        - a\\\\,D_{n-2}\\\\!\\\\left(x,\\\\, a\\\\right)\\\\quad (n \\\\ge 2),$$\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\"and satisfy the functional identity\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\"$$$D_n\\\\!\\\\left(u + \\\\frac{a}{u},\\\\, a\\\\right) = u^{n} + \\\\frac{a^{n}}{u^{n}}.$$\\n\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[],\"nodeType\":\"hr\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[{\"type\":\"bold\"}],\"value\":\"1. Choosing the polynomial\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\"Take $$a = 1$$ and $$n=19$$.\\n Because $$n$$ is odd, $$D_{19}\\\\!\\\\left(x,\\\\, 1\\\\right)$$ is an \",\"nodeType\":\"text\"},{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[{\"type\":\"bold\"}],\"value\":\"odd\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"},{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\" polynomial; the recurrence immediately shows it is \",\"nodeType\":\"text\"},{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[{\"type\":\"bold\"}],\"value\":\"monic\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"},{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\" and has \",\"nodeType\":\"text\"},{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[{\"type\":\"bold\"}],\"value\":\"integer (hence real) coefficients\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"},{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\".\\n The coefficient of $$x$$ is $$x = (-1)^{\\\\frac{n-1}{2}}\\\\,n = -19,$$ so all requested normalisations are satisfied.\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\"Writing it out gives\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\"$$$\\n\\\\boxed{%\\np(x) = D_{19}\\\\!\\\\left(x,1\\\\right)\\n       = x^{19} - 19x^{17} + 152x^{15} - 665x^{13}\\n       + 1729x^{11} - 2717x^{9} + 2508x^{7}\\n       - 1254x^{5} + 285x^{3} - 19x%\\n}\\\\,.\\n$$\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[],\"nodeType\":\"hr\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[{\"type\":\"bold\"}],\"value\":\"2. Irreducible components of $$fX \\\\;=\\\\; \\\\bigl\\\\{\\\\, p(x) = p(y) \\\\,\\\\bigr\\\\}$$\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\"For Dickson polynomials one has the classical factorisation\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\"$$$D_n\\\\!\\\\left(x,1\\\\right) \\\\;-\\\\; D_n\\\\!\\\\left(y,1\\\\right)\\n  \\\\;=\\\\;\\n  (x - y)\\\\,\\n  \\\\prod_{j=1}^{\\\\frac{n-1}{2}}\\n  \\\\Bigl(\\n        x^{2} + y^{2}\\n        - 2\\\\cos\\\\!\\\\bigl(\\\\tfrac{2\\\\pi j}{n}\\\\bigr)\\\\,xy\\n        - 4\\\\sin^{2}\\\\!\\\\bigl(\\\\tfrac{\\\\pi j}{n}\\\\bigr)\\n  \\\\Bigr),$$\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\"where each quadratic factor is irreducible over $$\\\\mathbb{C}$$ (see, e.g., Lidl–Niederreiter, \",\"nodeType\":\"text\"},{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[{\"type\":\"italic\"}],\"value\":\"Finite Fields\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"},{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\", §3.6).\\n For $$n=19$$ this gives\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[{\"type\":\"bold\"}],\"value\":\"one linear\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"},{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\" component $$x=y;$$\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"}],\"nodeType\":\"list-item\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[{\"type\":\"bold\"}],\"value\":\"nine quadratic\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"},{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\" components (corresponding to $$\\\\bigl(j = 1,\\\\,\\\\ldots,\\\\,9\\\\bigr).$$\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"}],\"nodeType\":\"list-item\"}],\"nodeType\":\"unordered-list\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\"Hence $$X \\\\subset \\\\mathbb{P}^{1} \\\\times \\\\mathbb{P}^{1}$$ has $$10$$ irreducible components, at least three of them and not all linear, as required.\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[],\"nodeType\":\"hr\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[{\"type\":\"bold\"}],\"value\":\"3. Evaluating $$p(19)$$\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\"Using the recurrence (or the identity $$D_n\\\\!\\\\left(x,\\\\,1\\\\right) \\\\;=\\\\; 2\\\\,T_n\\\\!\\\\bigl(\\\\tfrac{x}{2}\\\\bigr)$$ with Chebyshev $$T_n$$ one obtains\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\"$$$p(19) \\\\;=\\\\; D_{19}\\\\!\\\\left(19,\\\\,1\\\\right)\\n        \\\\;=\\\\; 1\\\\,876\\\\,572\\\\,071\\\\,974\\\\,094\\\\,803\\\\,391\\\\,179.$$\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[],\"nodeType\":\"hr\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\"Thus the polynomial above fulfils every condition, and\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\"$$$\\\\boxed{%\\np(19) \\\\;=\\\\; 1{,}876{,}572{,}071{,}974{,}094{,}803{,}391{,}179\\n}.$$\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"}],\"nodeType\":\"document\"},\"context\":{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\"The curve defined by p(x) = p(y) factors if p(x) is decomposable or has repeated factors. Actually, p(x)-p(y) always factors, and it’s divisible by (x-y). This gives one factor, and the rest is a degree d-1 polynomial in x and y. For the curve to have at least three irreducible components, p(x)-p(y) must factor into at least three nontrivial factors. Each factor corresponds to a component in projective space, and at least one must be nonlinear.\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"}],\"nodeType\":\"list-item\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\"I’m checking the Dickson polynomials D_n(x,a) and focusing on their coefficients. With a=1, it seems the leading coefficient is always 1, confirming that Dickson polynomials are monic. The linear coefficient for odd n is following a predictable sign pattern: for n=19, it gives a coefficient of -19, which is exactly what I need. So the Dickson polynomial D_n(x,1) with odd n fits nicely with the linear coefficient I was looking for.\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"}],\"nodeType\":\"list-item\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\"I've confirmed that the linear coefficient for p(x)=D19(x)p(x) = D_{19}(x)p(x)=D19​(x) is correct, with the necessary scaling factor to match. Now for the next step, I need to compute D19(19)D_{19}(19)D19​(19). Since Tn(z)T_n(z)Tn​(z) grows quickly for real z\u003e1z \u003e 1z\u003e1, I plan to compute this value using Python, leveraging the recurrence to avoid mistakes—it's going to be a big number. I'll make sure to present the numeric result neatly so it's understandable since the coefficients are integers.\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"}],\"nodeType\":\"list-item\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\"The Dickson polynomial D_{19}(x,1) is confirmed to have the required properties: monic, odd, real coefficients, and a linear coefficient of -19. The factorization of D_{19}(x) into irreducible components is also verified and satisfies the condition of having at least 10 components (1 linear + 9 quadratics). To compute p(19), we'll use the recurrence relation for Dickson polynomials, but there's also a closed-form expression involving cosine that could be handy here. I'll proceed accordingly!\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"}],\"nodeType\":\"list-item\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\"I confirmed the linear coefficient is working correctly, which means now I need to calculate p(19)=D19(19)p(19) = D_{19}(19)p(19)=D19​(19). This involves evaluating T19(9.5)T_{19}(9.5)T19​(9.5), which grows rapidly. To ensure I get an accurate result, I'll compute this using recurrence in Python. The result is a large number: 1,876,572,071,974,094,803,391,179. I'll use the commas to make it easier to read: p(19)=1,876,572,071,974,094,803,391,179p(19) = 1,876,572,071,974,094,803,391,179p(19)=1,876,572,071,974,094,803,391,179.\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"}],\"nodeType\":\"list-item\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\"For a = 1, there’s a relationship between Dickson polynomials and Chebyshev polynomials: D_n(x, 1) = 2^(n-1) T_n(x/2). Checking this, we see that the definition of D_n(u + 1/u) aligns well with Chebyshev’s formula. By setting x = 2 cos(θ), we find that D_n(x) equals 2 T_n(x/2). Importantly, the leading coefficient works out to 1, confirming the identity holds. This is nice!\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"}],\"nodeType\":\"list-item\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\"Now, I need to verify that p(x)−p(y)p(x) - p(y)p(x)−p(y) factors as desired, relying on the known Dickson polynomial factorization. According to the literature (like Fried and Jarden), the difference Dn(x)−Dn(y)D_n(x) - D_n(y)Dn​(x)−Dn​(y) factors into a product involving (x−y)(x - y)(x−y) and other expressions. I'll provide the explicit polynomial D19(x,1)D_{19}(x, 1)D19​(x,1), list the coefficients, and explain how it satisfies the desired properties: monic, odd, etc. Lastly, I'll compute p(19)p(19)p(19).\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"}],\"nodeType\":\"list-item\"}],\"nodeType\":\"unordered-list\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\"Analyzed\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"},{\"data\":{\"target\":{\"metadata\":{\"tags\":[],\"concepts\":[]},\"sys\":{\"id\":\"4iDqj8yln1uJDaqNPHyID6\",\"type\":\"Entry\",\"publishedVersion\":131,\"revision\":33,\"contentType\":{\"sys\":{\"type\":\"Link\",\"linkType\":\"ContentType\",\"id\":\"componentCodeExample\"}},\"locale\":\"en-US\"},\"fields\":{\"language\":\"python\",\"code\":\"from math import comb, cos, acos\\n# Let's compute Dickson D_n(x,a=1) recursively\\ndef dickson(n,x,a=1):\\n    if n==0:\\n        return 2\\n    if n==1:\\n        return x\\n    Dm2=2\\n    Dm1=x\\n    for k in range(2,n+1):\\n        D= x*Dm1 - a*Dm2\\n        Dm2, Dm1 = Dm1, D\\n    return Dm1\\n\\nd19 = dickson(19,19,1)\\nd19\",\"caption\":\"1876572071974094803391179\"}}},\"content\":[],\"nodeType\":\"embedded-entry-block\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\"Analyzed\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"},{\"data\":{\"target\":{\"metadata\":{\"tags\":[],\"concepts\":[]},\"sys\":{\"id\":\"7f590284Uopcg3jebHymPi\",\"type\":\"Entry\",\"publishedVersion\":131,\"revision\":33,\"contentType\":{\"sys\":{\"type\":\"Link\",\"linkType\":\"ContentType\",\"id\":\"componentCodeExample\"}},\"locale\":\"en-US\"},\"fields\":{\"language\":\"python\",\"code\":\"d19\",\"caption\":\"1876572071974094803391179\"}}},\"content\":[],\"nodeType\":\"embedded-entry-block\"},{\"data\":{},\"content\":[{\"data\":{},\"marks\":[],\"value\":\"Analyzed\",\"nodeType\":\"text\"}],\"nodeType\":\"paragraph\"},{\"data\":{\"target\":{\"metadata\":{\"tags\":[],\"